---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { services, proof } from "../data/services";
---

<Layout
  title="17th Street Labs | AI engineering, evals and security"
  description="A founder-led AI engineering and security firm. We build agentic products, prove they work with real evaluations, and find how they break before someone else does."
>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="container hero-grid">
      <div class="hero-copy">
        <h1>AI systems that hold up in production.</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          17th Street Labs is a small, senior engineering and security firm. We
          build agentic products, prove they work with real evaluations, and
          find how they break before someone else does.
        </p>
        <div class="hero-actions">
          <a href="/contact/" data-contact-dialog class="button button-primary">
            Bring us the hard problem
          </a>
          <a class="button button-ghost" href="/services/">See what we do</a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <figure class="boundary-figure" aria-hidden="true">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 520 440" class="boundary-diagram" fill="none">
          <defs>
            <marker id="arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse">
              <path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 z" fill="currentColor" />
            </marker>
          </defs>
          <g class="d-ink">
            <rect x="24" y="24" width="472" height="392" rx="6" stroke-dasharray="6 6" />
            <text x="40" y="52" class="d-label">trust boundary</text>
            <rect x="60" y="180" width="150" height="80" rx="4" />
            <text x="135" y="215" text-anchor="middle" class="d-title">agent</text>
            <text x="135" y="236" text-anchor="middle" class="d-label">plans · chooses · acts</text>
            <rect x="330" y="80" width="140" height="56" rx="4" />
            <text x="400" y="113" text-anchor="middle" class="d-title">tools</text>
            <rect x="330" y="192" width="140" height="56" rx="4" />
            <text x="400" y="225" text-anchor="middle" class="d-title">data</text>
            <rect x="330" y="304" width="140" height="56" rx="4" />
            <text x="400" y="337" text-anchor="middle" class="d-title">people</text>
            <rect x="60" y="330" width="150" height="56" rx="4" />
            <text x="135" y="363" text-anchor="middle" class="d-title">user</text>
            <path d="M135 330 V260" marker-end="url(#arrow)" />
            <path d="M210 205 C270 205 270 108 330 108" marker-end="url(#arrow)" />
            <path d="M210 220 H330" marker-end="url(#arrow)" />
            <path d="M210 235 C270 235 270 332 330 332" marker-end="url(#arrow)" />
          </g>
          <g class="d-accent">
            <path d="M296 60 V400" />
            <rect x="272" y="188" width="48" height="24" rx="12" fill="currentColor" />
            <text x="296" y="204" text-anchor="middle" class="d-badge">eval</text>
            <rect x="272" y="76" width="48" height="24" rx="12" fill="currentColor" />
            <text x="296" y="92" text-anchor="middle" class="d-badge">allow</text>
            <rect x="272" y="300" width="48" height="24" rx="12" fill="currentColor" />
            <text x="296" y="316" text-anchor="middle" class="d-badge">deny</text>
            <circle class="d-pulse" cx="230" cy="220" r="4" fill="currentColor" />
          </g>
        </svg>
        <figcaption>
          Once software can plan, pick tools, and act for someone, every
          interface is a trust boundary. We make each one explicit, tested, and
          hard to cross.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
    <div class="container">
      <dl class="proof-strip">
        {proof.map(({ value, label }) => (
          <div>
            <dt>{value}</dt>
            <dd>{label}</dd>
          </div>
        ))}
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container section-grid">
      <div class="section-intro">
        <h2>What we do</h2>
        <p>
          Four ways in. Most engagements start with one and grow into another
          once the first result lands.
        </p>
      </div>
      <ol class="service-list">
        {services.map((service) => (
          <li>
            <a href={`/services/#${service.slug}`}>
              <h3>{service.title}</h3>
              <p>{service.summary}</p>
              <span class="outcome">{service.outcome}</span>
            </a>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-tint">
    <div class="container section-grid">
      <div class="section-intro">
        <h2>Work you can inspect</h2>
        <p>
          We publish our research so you can judge the quality of our thinking
          before you hire us.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="research">
        <h3>ExploitHunter</h3>
        <p class="research-lede">
          An open-source, local-first lab for studying how AI models discover,
          validate, and report security findings across long-running attack
          workflows. It doubles as the evaluation harness we use on client
          work.
        </p>
        <dl class="research-stats">
          <div><dt>412M+</dt><dd>tokens evaluated</dd></div>
          <div><dt>173</dt><dd>model profiles compared</dd></div>
          <div><dt>4,835</dt><dd>scored evaluation rows</dd></div>
        </dl>
        <ul class="plain-list">
          <li>Deterministic and LLM judges, side by side</li>
          <li>Trajectory, evidence, cost, and tool telemetry per run</li>
          <li>Reproducible regression gates you can put in CI</li>
        </ul>
        <a class="text-link" href="https://exploithunter.app" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">
          Explore ExploitHunter
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container section-grid">
      <div class="section-intro">
        <h2>How an engagement starts</h2>
        <p>Three steps, in this order. No discovery theater.</p>
      </div>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li>
          <h3>Frame the question that matters</h3>
          <p>
            We name the decision, failure mode, or capability at stake and
            agree on what success looks like before anyone writes code.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Build the smallest credible proof</h3>
          <p>
            Architecture, research, and evaluation move together so evidence
            arrives early enough to change direction cheaply.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Harden what survives, then hand it over</h3>
          <p>
            We pressure-test the system, turn findings into controls, and leave
            your team able to run it without us.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-tint">
    <div class="container section-grid">
      <div class="section-intro">
        <h2>Who you work with</h2>
        <p>Two founders. The people you meet are the people who do the work.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="founder-pair">
        <div class="founder">
          <h3>Dan Levy</h3>
          <p class="role">Co-founder, principal AI engineer</p>
          <p>
            Twenty-five years across production systems, developer platforms,
            AI applications, evaluation infrastructure, and offensive and
            defensive AI security. Educator and former head of a 60-person
            platform organization.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="founder">
          <h3>Marina Levy</h3>
          <p class="role">Co-founder, chief executive</p>
          <p>
            Runs the firm and every engagement's shape: scope, cadence, and the
            measurable outcome we are accountable for. Keeps ambitious
            technical work pointed at client value.
          </p>
        </div>
        <a class="text-link" href="/about/">More about the firm</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="closing">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>Tell us what is at stake.</h2>
      <p>
        Send a few sentences. You will hear back from Dan, usually within a
        business day.
      </p>
      <a href="/contact/" data-contact-dialog class="button button-primary">
        Start a conversation
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
